{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"OBSERVATION\n`review_plan_start` records a local `.rebar/gate_runs/<job_id>` entry as `status=running`, then starts the gate on a background daemon thread (`src/rebar/_mcp_llm.py:_start_gate_job` -> `_spawn_gate_daemon`). `gate_status` (`src/rebar/llm/gate_runs.py:gate_run_status`) resolves a still-`running` index to `stale-running` when `_mcp_inflight.is_job_active(job_id)` is false and the run is older than the 5s grace. That matches the reported job: a running handle became `stale-running`, findings were `run-stale-running`, and no readable `REVIEW_RESULT` or plan-review attestation existed.\n\nHYPOTHESIS\nThe result is lost because the async gate worker is only an in-process daemon thread (`threading.Thread(..., daemon=True)` in `_spawn_gate_daemon`). If the MCP process exits/restarts/is killed before the thread reaches its `finally`, the terminal `record_gate_run(...)`, `handle.complete(...)`, `REVIEW_RESULT`, and signature never happen. A later poll in a fresh process has the stale local `running` index but no live in-flight registry entry, so `gate_run_status` reports `stale-running` rather than an actionable terminal error.\n\nCONFIRMATION\nI reproduced the mechanism without a billable model call in a scratch repo outside the checkout: initialized a rebar store, called `_start_gate_job('plan_review', ..., work=lambda: sleep(60))`, let the Python process exit immediately, slept past `_STALE_GRACE_SECONDS`, then polled `gate_runs.gate_run_status(job_id)` from a new process. It returned `{'status': 'stale-running', 'findings': {'readable': False, 'reason': 'run-stale-running'}, 'durable': ... unsigned/error}`. This would have been disproven if the daemon survived process exit, if the terminal `finally` record appeared, or if durable findings/signature existed. The exact production reason the MCP process died is not encoded in current durable evidence; the reproduced root mechanism is the in-process daemon durability gap.\n\nSUGGESTED FIX DIRECTION\nDo not rely on daemon threads for long gate durability. Move async gates to a process/durable job model with heartbeat + terminal error sidecar/reaper, or at minimum write a recoverable diagnostic terminal record when a running handle goes stale so callers see why no result exists and can safely retry."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788737320069302001,"uuid":"a88dd6c1-d84e-497b-8cca-07369fad0172"}